<GuideLayout
  title="Knitting Gauge Explained"
  description="What knitting gauge means, how to measure a swatch, and how to resize pattern stitch counts when your gauge differs."
  path="/guides/knitting-gauge-explained/"
  answerFirst="Gauge is how many stitches and rows you get in 4 inches (10 cm). If a pattern expects 20 stitches per 4 inches and you get 18, your fabric will be wider unless you change needles or resize stitch counts. Measure a blocked swatch, then use our gauge calculator to convert stitches to inches and adjust patterns."
>
  <h2>Why gauge matters</h2>
  <p>
    Width is controlled by stitch gauge; length by row gauge. A sweater that is 5% too wide across the chest can jump a full size.
    Accessories are more forgiving, but blankets still drift when stitch counts are wrong.
  </p>
  <h2>How to swatch</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Cast on enough stitches for at least 5 inches of fabric in pattern stitch.</li>
    <li>Work at least 5 inches tall; bind off loosely.</li>
    <li>Wash and block as you will the finished project.</li>
    <li>Measure 4 inches in the center; count stitches and rows.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Use the <a href="/tools/knitting-gauge-calculator/">knitting gauge calculator</a> if your swatch is not exactly 4 inches.</p>
  <h2>Resizing a pattern</h2>
  <p>
    New stitch count ≈ pattern stitches × (your stitches per 4" ÷ pattern stitches per 4").
    Keep lace and cable repeats intact—adjust plain stockinette regions when possible.
    Pair with the <a href="/tools/cast-on-calculator/">cast-on calculator</a> and
    <a href="/tools/increase-decrease-calculator/">even increase/decrease tool</a>.
  </p>
  <h2>Needle changes</h2>
  <p>
    Larger needles usually lower stitches per inch. Convert sizes with the
    <a href="/tools/needle-hook-size-converter/">needle size converter</a> and swatch again—do not assume a one-size jump fixes gauge.
  </p>
  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="https://www.craftyarncouncil.com/standards">Craft Yarn Council standards</a></li>
  </ul>
</GuideLayout>
